    "ogHeadline": "AFRO SAMURAI WAS THE ONE\nHIP-HOP ANIME THAT LANDED",
    "ogAccent": "F2E600",
    "heroImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a9/Samuel_L._Jackson_2019_by_Glenn_Francis.jpg",
    "heroCredit": "Glenn Francis / Wikimedia Commons",
    "heroCreditUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Samuel_L._Jackson_2019_by_Glenn_Francis.jpg",
    "heroFocus": "center top",
    "trendScore": 56,
    "body": "*Afro Samurai* is the only project that ever built a hip-hop anime from the inside out rather than bolting a rap song onto a trailer. A Japanese artist drew a Black samurai with an afro. A Japanese studio animated it. A Black American actor produced it and voiced both leads. A Black American producer scored every frame of it. It premiered on Spike TV on January 4, 2007, and in the nineteen years since, nobody has put those four things in the same room again.\n\n## Okazaki drew it for himself first\n\nThe source is not a pitch deck. Takashi Okazaki serialized [*Afro Samurai*](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Afro_Samurai) as a dōjinshi — self-published manga — in the indie magazine *Nou Nou Hau* starting in 1998, drawn out of his own appetite for American hip-hop and blaxploitation and samurai film. That matters to the final product. The fusion wasn't a market study; it was one artist's record collection rendered in ink, which is why the design language never feels like a costume.\n\nThe animation came from Gonzo, directed by Fuminori Kizaki, and the five-episode run carried a visual grammar closer to a Hype Williams video than a Sunday morning block: high contrast, slow-motion arterial spray, a title character who moves like he's counting bars.\n\n## Jackson played the conscience and the clown\n\nSamuel L. Jackson executive produced and voiced Afro and Ninja Ninja, the motormouth apparition who follows him everywhere. Splitting one man into the silent killer and the running commentary is the show's real formal idea, and it is an old Black-storytelling structure — the signifying second voice — dropped into a chanbara frame. [NPR](https://www.npr.org/2007/01/04/6718825/tvs-afro-samurai-fuses-martial-arts-hip-hop) covered the premiere that week as a genuine martial-arts-and-hip-hop fusion rather than a novelty, which at the time was not the consensus read.\n\nThe cast around Jackson — Kelly Hu, Ron Perlman, Phil LaMarr — was a Los Angeles voice room, not a stunt-casting exercise, and the English version was the primary version. That is rare. Most anime reaching an American audience is a dub of a finished object. This was built for the American recording booth first.\n\n## RZA scored it like a Wu album\n\nRZA did not license songs to *Afro Samurai*. He scored it, and then released [*Afro Samurai: The Album*](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Afro_Samurai:_The_Album) through his own Wu Music Group in January 2007 — dusty loops, vocal features, the same sword-and-code obsession he had been running since *Enter the Wu-Tang*. It was the logical endpoint of a 14-year project: the man who built an entire rap cosmology out of dubbed martial-arts cinema finally got to score the cartoon instead of sampling one.\n\nHe had already done the dry run on [*Ghost Dog: The Way of the Samurai*](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghost_Dog:_The_Way_of_the_Samurai_(soundtrack)) in 1999 for Jim Jarmusch. *Afro Samurai* was the version where the imagery and the score came from the same place.\n\n## Why nothing matched it\n\n*Afro Samurai: Resurrection* followed on January 25, 2009 with Lucy Liu added and RZA back on the boards, and then the model stopped. Not because it failed — because the conditions were unusual. You needed a Japanese creator whose source material was already Black-coded, a studio willing to animate it straight, a movie star willing to executive produce a five-episode cable anime, and a producer who could score 25 minutes of screen time without reaching for a needle drop.\n\nWhat the industry did instead was cheaper: put a rapper on the ending theme, license a character to a sneaker, sell the crossover without building one. The collab economy that followed — anime on hoodies, anime on Chuck Taylors, anime on tour merch — extracts the aesthetic without commissioning the work. *Afro Samurai* commissioned the work.\n\n## The take\n\nOwnership is the difference. Okazaki owned the character before anyone bought it. Jackson produced rather than just voiced. RZA released the score on his own label rather than handing it to a studio music department. That is three Black-and-independent equity positions in a mainstream anime production, and it is still the only time the stack lined up that way. Every anime-and-hip-hop crossover since has been a licensing deal. The one that mattered was a co-production, and the culture has been waiting nineteen years for the sequel to that arrangement, not to that story.\n\n## Sources\n- [Afro Samurai](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Afro_Samurai) — Wikipedia, accessed 2026\n- [TV's 'Afro Samurai' Fuses Martial Arts, Hip-Hop](https://www.npr.org/2007/01/04/6718825/tvs-afro-samurai-fuses-martial-arts-hip-hop) — NPR, January 4, 2007\n- [Afro Samurai: The Album](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Afro_Samurai:_The_Album) — Wikipedia, accessed 2026\n- [Afro Samurai: Resurrection](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Afro_Samurai:_Resurrection) — Wikipedia, accessed 2026\n- [Ghost Dog: The Way of the Samurai (soundtrack)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghost_Dog:_The_Way_of_the_Samurai_(soundtrack)) — Wikipedia, accessed 2026\n",
