    "ogHeadline": "THE AIR JORDAN 3\nWAS A RETENTION OFFER",
    "ogAccent": "F58426",
    "heroImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Air_Jordan_III_White_Cement_Reimagined.jpg/1920px-Air_Jordan_III_White_Cement_Reimagined.jpg",
    "heroCredit": "HI 622 / Wikimedia Commons",
    "heroCreditUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Air_Jordan_III_White_Cement_Reimagined.jpg",
    "heroFocus": "center",
    "trendScore": 58,
    "body": "The Air Jordan 3 is usually told as a design story. It was a personnel story. In 1987 the two men who had signed Michael Jordan and built his first two shoes, Peter Moore and Rob Strasser, quit Nike and started recruiting him for their own venture. Jordan's contract was up. The shoe Tinker Hatfield showed him was the reason he stayed, and every Jordan retro since has been printed on the paper Hatfield used that day.\n\n## The room\n\n[Highsnobiety](https://www.highsnobiety.com/p/air-jordan-3-guide/)'s history sets the stakes: Moore and Strasser had departed to form their own company and \"attempting to recruit Jordan away,\" and the 3 presentation was Nike's counter. Hatfield had been an architect designing Nike's buildings until 1985; the Air Max 1 was his first shoe. He asked Jordan what he wanted. What came back was a mid-cut, softer leather, no big Swoosh, something he could wear off the court without looking like he'd come from practice. The shoe launched in January 1988 at $100.\n\n## What the AJ2 had already solved\n\nThe part that gets erased is that the 3's platform was built by someone else. Bruce Kilgore, the Air Force 1's designer, led the Air Jordan 2 with Mark Parker, per [Boardroom](https://boardroom.tv/the-air-jordan-2-an-oral-history/), and that shoe's Italian construction was the first Jordan with full-length encapsulated Air and a lowered stack height. [Nike](https://www.nike.com/gb/a/air-jordan-2-history) credits Kilgore alongside Moore on the 2. The AJ3's mid-cut, its luxury-leather ambitions and its refusal to wear a Swoosh on the side are all AJ2 ideas that the market rejected in 1986. Hatfield kept the ideas and changed the personality. Kilgore's name is on the Air Ship, the AF1 and the AJ2, and rarely on the 3, and that omission flatters the wrong narrative about how the line evolved.\n\n## Elephant print, visible Air, a Jumpman\n\nThe 3 made three moves. The grey elephant-hide print on the toe and heel gave the shoe a texture no basketball sneaker had used. The visible heel Air unit, carried over from the Air Max 1, put Nike's technology on display. And the tongue got the Jumpman, the silhouette Moore had drawn in 1987 from a Life magazine photo, making the 3 the first Jordan to wear the logo that would replace the Wings. Highsnobiety notes Jordan first laced them up in November 1987, but the coming-out was the February 1988 All-Star Weekend in Chicago, where he took the dunk contest from the free-throw line in Black Cement 3s.\n\n## Mars sold it\n\nNike's agency, Wieden+Kennedy, had Jim Riswold and Bill Davenport pitch a campaign built on Spike Lee's Mars Blackmon, and Lee directed the first two spots in December 1987, per [Complex](https://www.complex.com/pop-culture/a/russ-bengtson/spike-lee-mars-blackmon-history). The line was \"It's gotta be the shoes.\" The 3 was the first Jordan sold with a Black director's voice attached, and the pairing of Hatfield's shoe with Lee's character is the reason the Jumpman means anything to people who never watched Jordan play.\n\nThe timing matters. Lee shot the first spots the month before the shoe launched and the same season Jordan's contract was in question. Nike wasn't just selling a sneaker in early 1988; it was selling Jordan on the idea that the company understood his audience better than the men who had just left it. Mars was the proof of concept.\n\n## The retro record\n\nThe Black Cement and White Cement came back in 1994, 2001, 2008, 2011 and 2018, with the 2018 pair the first since 2001 to carry Nike Air on the heel, per Highsnobiety. A [women's Fireside 3 lands this October](https://sneakzandbeatz.com/the-lane/air-jordan-3-wmns-fireside-ck9246-200-official-photos-october-3-travis-look-2026). The silhouette has been in continuous production for most of the past three decades, which is the real measure of the pitch: Jordan didn't just stay, he signed on to a shoe that outlived his career.\n\n## The take\n\nNike kept Michael Jordan in 1988 because it listened to him for the first time. The 3 is the first Jordan that reads as the athlete's taste instead of the company's guess, and the reason it worked is that Hatfield treated a 24-year-old Black man's sense of style as the design brief. Everything Jordan Brand sells now, $7 billion a year of retros, stands on that shift.\n\nThe people who built the platform got less. Kilgore's AJ2 is a footnote in the 3's story, Moore's Jumpman replaced his own Wings, and Strasser was dead by 1993. The shoe that kept Jordan was assembled from other men's work and a Black director's ad campaign, and the party that owns all of it is the one that nearly lost him.\n\n## Sources\n- [The Complete History of the Nike Air Jordan 3](https://www.highsnobiety.com/p/air-jordan-3-guide/) — Highsnobiety, January 29, 2019\n- [The Air Jordan 2: An Oral History](https://boardroom.tv/the-air-jordan-2-an-oral-history/) — Boardroom, December 29, 2022\n- [The History of the Air Jordan 2](https://www.nike.com/gb/a/air-jordan-2-history) — Nike, undated\n- [Spike Lee, Mars Blackmon and 'She's Gotta Have It'](https://www.complex.com/pop-culture/a/russ-bengtson/spike-lee-mars-blackmon-history) — Complex, November 24, 2017\n",
