    "slug": "sonny-vaccaro-converse-adidas-michael-jordan-1984-counterfactual",
    "pillar": "sneakers",
    "title": "Michael Jordan Wanted adidas. adidas Passed.",
    "headline": "Michael Jordan Wanted adidas.\nadidas Passed.",
    "subhead": "He wore Converse at Carolina and wanted three stripes as a pro. Sonny Vaccaro, George Raveling and Deloris Jordan got him into a Nike conference room instead. The counterfactual is the whole industry.",
    "description": "The 1984 counterfactual: Michael Jordan wanted adidas, wore Converse at Carolina, and signed with Nike only after Sonny Vaccaro and his mother pushed.",
    "keywords": [
      "Sonny Vaccaro",
      "adidas",
      "Converse",
      "Michael Jordan 1984",
      "George Raveling",
      "Deloris Jordan",
      "Air film",
      "Nike",
      "ABCD Camp",
      "Kobe Bryant adidas",
      "Rob Strasser",
      "Sneakz and Beatz",
      "PHRHX",
      "Black-owned media"
    ],
    "ogHeadline": "MICHAEL JORDAN WANTED ADIDAS.\nADIDAS PASSED.",
    "ogAccent": "F58426",
    "heroImage": "https://sneakerbardetroit.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/Air-Jordan-1-High-85-Bred-HV6674-067-Release-Date.jpg",
    "heroCredit": "Sneaker Bar Detroit",
    "heroCreditUrl": "https://sneakerbardetroit.com/air-jordan-1-high-85-bred-2025/",
    "heroFocus": "center",
    "trendScore": 55,
    "body": "The most valuable shoe brand on earth exists because a German company could not be bothered. In the summer of 1984, Michael Jordan wanted to sign with adidas. He had worn Converse for four years at North Carolina, as every Dean Smith player did, and he liked the three stripes for the same reason every kid in Wilmington did: they looked better. adidas, per [ESPN's Darren Rovell](https://www.espn.com/nba/story/_/id/15463041/michael-jordan-agent-tries-set-record-straight-original-nike-deal), \"showed minimal interest.\" Converse offered $100,000 a year and a spot on a roster with Magic and Bird. Nike offered a line with his name on it. He took the one he did not want.\n\n## Vaccaro's pitch\n\nSonny Vaccaro was a Pennsylvania hustler who had co-founded the Dapper Dan Roundball Classic in 1965 and walked into Nike in 1977 with a shoe idea; what he actually sold them was the strategy of paying college coaches to put their players in Nike, per [Wikipedia's account of his career](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sonny_Vaccaro). By 1984 he was the company's basketball whisperer, and his read on the draft was that Nike should stop spreading its basketball budget across the class and spend all of it on the third pick. Rob Strasser and Phil Knight went along. The deal took more than three months to close, [TIME](https://time.com/6268621/air-true-story-behind-movie/) reports, and Vaccaro has called it \"my greatest journey.\"\n\nNike's problem was that Jordan would not take the meeting. That is where the two other names come in. George Raveling, the Black assistant on Bob Knight's 1984 Olympic staff and a Nike man, was the one telling Jordan to at least hear Beaverton out; Jordan himself has said Raveling deserves the credit. And Deloris Jordan, per [Bleacher Report](https://bleacherreport.com/articles/2891202-david-falk-michael-jordans-nike-contract-the-best-worst-deal-ive-ever-made), told her son he was getting on the plane to Oregon whether he wanted to or not. James Jordan told him afterward it was \"a great deal.\" The 2023 film \"Air\" cast Viola Davis as Deloris and gave her the closing negotiation. In the actual paperwork, David Falk wrote the royalty. In the actual family, Deloris got him in the room.\n\n## What adidas was thinking\n\nadidas in 1984 was a Herzogenaurach family company still reorganizing after founder Adi Dassler's death in 1978, with a U.S. basketball business built around Kareem Abdul-Jabbar and not much of a plan beyond him. It did not believe a rookie guard could carry a line. Nike did not believe it either; it had budgeted $3 million in sales by year four and got $126 million in year one, per Bleacher Report. The difference is that Nike was desperate. Its running business was slipping and it needed a new category. Desperation, not vision, is why the Jumpman is a Swoosh.\n\nVaccaro would spend the rest of his career proving the point from the other side. He left Nike in 1991, joined adidas in 1992, and signed Kobe Bryant there out of Lower Merion in 1996, giving the three stripes the signature star it had passed on twelve years earlier. When adidas would not match his pitch for LeBron James in 2003, LeBron went to Nike and Vaccaro went to Reebok. Then he left the business and spent his last act recruiting Ed O'Bannon for the lawsuit that broke the NCAA's amateurism rules. The man who got Jordan paid spent his retirement getting college kids paid.\n\n## The counterfactual\n\nSuppose adidas had said yes. Jordan gets a three-stripe signature, probably a version of the Forum or Top Ten with his name on the tongue, and a flat fee, because adidas did not do royalties. There is no Air Jordan 1, no \"Banned\" ad, no Tinker Hatfield 3 and therefore no reason for Jordan to stay in 1987 when he was ready to leave Nike. There is no Jordan Brand in 1997, because adidas would not have spun out a division for one athlete, and no $7 billion sub-brand today. Converse, which Nike bought out of bankruptcy in 2003, might have stayed independent on the strength of the Magic-Bird-Jordan roster. And Nike, without the basketball business that saved it in 1985, is a running-shoe company that Reebok probably eats by 1990.\n\nThe culture is different too. The 1s that Bed-Stuy and the South Side wore through the late 1980s would have been Forums. The tuxedo shoe would have been a Torsion. Hip-hop's relationship with the Swoosh, from Spike and Mars to Nas's Breds, would be a relationship with three stripes, and Run-DMC's \"My Adidas\" would be the beginning of a dynasty instead of a detour.\n\n## The take\n\nJordan wanted adidas, Vaccaro wanted Jordan, Raveling wanted his friend to listen, and Deloris wanted her son to be smart about money. Nike got the benefit of all four and paid one of them. The counterfactual is worth sitting with because it shows how thin the margin was: the largest transfer of Black cultural value to a single corporation in the history of sports came down to a German company's indifference and a mother's insistence. Neither of those is in the Nike annual report.\n\n## Sources\n- [Michael Jordan's agent tries to set record straight on original Nike deal](https://www.espn.com/nba/story/_/id/15463041/michael-jordan-agent-tries-set-record-straight-original-nike-deal) — ESPN, May 4, 2016\n- [The True Story Behind 'Air'](https://time.com/6268621/air-true-story-behind-movie/) — TIME, April 5, 2023\n- [Sonny Vaccaro](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sonny_Vaccaro) — Wikipedia\n- [David Falk: Michael Jordan's Nike Contract the Best, Worst Deal I've Ever Made](https://bleacherreport.com/articles/2891202-david-falk-michael-jordans-nike-contract-the-best-worst-deal-ive-ever-made) — Bleacher Report, May 11, 2020\n",
