    "slug": "union-la-a-ma-maniere-black-owned-boutique-jordan-collab-run",
    "pillar": "sneakers",
    "title": "Who Gets the Call From Beaverton, and Why",
    "headline": "Who Gets the Call\nFrom Beaverton, and Why",
    "subhead": "Union LA and A Ma Maniére have run the strongest boutique Jordan program of the decade. The shoes are the visible part. The stores, the sizing and the stories are the actual product.",
    "description": "Chris Gibbs' Union LA and James Whitner's A Ma Maniére run the decade's strongest boutique Jordan program. Why Black-owned stores get the call from Beaverton.",
    "keywords": [
      "Union LA Air Jordan 4",
      "Chris Gibbs",
      "A Ma Maniere Air Jordan 3",
      "Raised by Women",
      "James Whitner",
      "Whitaker Group",
      "While You Were Sleeping Air Jordan 4",
      "FZ4810-001",
      "DH3434-110",
      "Bephies Beauty Supply",
      "Black-owned sneaker boutiques",
      "Sneakz and Beatz",
      "PHRHX",
      "Black-owned media"
    ],
    "ogHeadline": "WHO GETS THE CALL\nFROM BEAVERTON, AND WHY",
    "ogAccent": "C8262C",
    "heroImage": "https://sneakernews.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/a-ma-maniere-air-jordan-4-while-you-were-sleeping-fz4810-001-release-date-1.jpg",
    "heroCredit": "A Ma Maniére via Sneaker News",
    "heroCreditUrl": "https://sneakernews.com/2024/09/10/a-ma-maniere-air-jordan-4-while-you-were-sleeping-fz4810-001-release-date/",
    "heroFocus": "center",
    "trendScore": 58,
    "body": "Jordan Brand doesn't hand its first four silhouettes to retailers. It hands them to people who can make the shoe mean something the brand can't say itself. In the past eight years, the two boutique owners who have done that most consistently are Chris Gibbs at Union in Los Angeles and James Whitner at A Ma Maniére in Atlanta, and the pattern of what they've been given, when, and how they've used it, is the clearest available answer to who gets the call from Beaverton.\n\n## Union: the AJ1 in 2018, then the 4\n\nUnion's Jordan program opened with the Air Jordan 1 High OG in 2018, $225, in \"Black Toe\" and \"Storm Blue,\" per [SoleSavy's archive](https://news.solesavy.com/every-union-nike-jordan-sneaker-collab/). The stitched-together, two-shoes-in-one look set the language. Then came the 4, revealed August 6, 2020, in \"Off Noir\" (DC9533-001) and \"Guava Ice.\" Matt Welty's [Complex](https://www.complex.com/sneakers/a/matt-welty/union-air-jordan-4-sneaker-collaboration) piece has Gibbs on the folded tongue: \"I had always liked to wear my IV's with a short tongue.\" The stitches were removable, so the tongue could go back to full length. He also re-cut the paneling and added a mesh toe. The 4, he said, is his second-favorite Jordan.\n\nThe run continued: \"Taupe Haze\" and \"Desert Moss\" 4s in 2021, the Air Jordan 2 on April 15, 2022, at $225, an Air Jordan 1 Low KO on February 2, 2023, at $150, and on August 24, 2023, a his-and-hers AJ1 with Bephies Beauty Supply, the brand Beth Birkett Gibbs founded in 2020 for Black women in beauty and fashion, per [Andscape](https://andscape.com/features/union-bephies-beauty-supply-air-jordan-1-chris-gibbs-beth-gibbs-interview/). Five silhouettes in five years, all from the first two decades of the catalog, all with a Black-owned store's name on the box.\n\n## A Ma Maniére: the 3, then the 4, sized for everyone\n\nWhitner runs the Whitaker Group, parent of A Ma Maniére, Social Status and APB, and his first Jordan collab was the Air Jordan 3 \"Raised by Women,\" released April 21, 2021, style DH3434-110. Ross Dwyer's [Hypebeast](https://hypebeast.com/2021/4/a-ma-maniere-air-jordan-3-raised-by-women-release-date-james-whitner-interview) interview has the argument: \"The idea of segregated gender design is antiquated.\" The shoe carried extended size runs across genders, quotes from Whitner's mother handwritten under the heel tabs, and a women's-first raffle. It came with a short film about the Black women who raised him. Whitner: \"You have to be accessible to the community.\"\n\nThe 4 followed on September 20, 2024, at $225, style FZ4810-001, per [Sneaker News](https://sneakernews.com/2024/09/10/a-ma-maniere-air-jordan-4-while-you-were-sleeping-fz4810-001-release-date/): a Phantom base with Violet Ore and Burgundy Crush, a pinched ankle and tongue, quilted lining, \"Work Harder\" on the lace tips and the A Ma Maniére logo on the left tongue only. The size run went from W5 to W16.5. An in-store raffle opened in Houston the day before. The Whitaker Group's stores got the shoe first, in their own cities, on their own terms. That template now extends to APB and JAIDE, whose [11 Lows](https://sneakzandbeatz.com/the-lane/apb-air-jordan-11-low-binary-blue-iu8266-400-september-18-whitaker-group-2026) ran through [Whitaker Group doors, not SNKRS](https://sneakzandbeatz.com/the-lane/jaide-air-jordan-11-low-fiberglass-iw1882-300-september-18-whitaker-group-womens-2026).\n\n## What Beaverton is buying\n\nLook at what both programs share. A physical store with a clientele Jordan Brand can't reach through an app. A founder whose story fits on a heel tab. A design edit small enough to keep the silhouette recognizable and specific enough to be defended in an interview. And a distribution plan that routes the first pairs through the boutique's own doors, in Black neighborhoods, before anyone else.\n\nThat last part is the trade. Jordan Brand gets credibility it cannot manufacture and a story its own copywriters can't write. The boutique gets a run of the most valuable silhouettes on earth and the foot traffic that comes with them. The shoe is the artifact. The store is the asset.\n\n## The take\n\nThe Union and A Ma Maniére runs are the strongest ownership story in the Jordan catalog because the owners kept the thing that matters: the store, the brand, the customer relationship. Gibbs' tongue and Whitner's sizing are design decisions, but they're also proof that Black retailers can direct a Jordan release rather than just stock it.\n\nThe asymmetry hasn't gone anywhere. Jordan Brand chooses who gets the call, sets the quantities and owns the mold. What Gibbs and Whitner have built is the leverage to negotiate inside that: a women's-first raffle, a his-and-hers pair, a Houston in-store before the app. The call from Beaverton goes to people who already own something. That's the lesson, and it's the same one Michael Jordan learned in 1984.\n\n## Sources\n- [The Complete History of Union x Nike Collabs](https://news.solesavy.com/every-union-nike-jordan-sneaker-collab/) — SoleSavy, August 21, 2023\n- [You're Going to Like the Union x Air Jordan IVs, Even If You Hated Them](https://www.complex.com/sneakers/a/matt-welty/union-air-jordan-4-sneaker-collaboration) — Complex, August 6, 2020\n- [One love: How the summer of 1996 inspired Union's latest Air Jordan collaboration](https://andscape.com/features/union-bephies-beauty-supply-air-jordan-1-chris-gibbs-beth-gibbs-interview/) — Andscape, August 8, 2023\n- [A Ma Maniére's Air Jordan 3 \"Raised By Women\" Defies Sneaker Gender Barriers](https://hypebeast.com/2021/4/a-ma-maniere-air-jordan-3-raised-by-women-release-date-james-whitner-interview) — Hypebeast, April 15, 2021\n- [A Ma Maniére Air Jordan 4 \"While You Were Sleeping\"](https://sneakernews.com/2024/09/10/a-ma-maniere-air-jordan-4-while-you-were-sleeping-fz4810-001-release-date/) — Sneaker News, September 10, 2024\n",
