    "slug": "rza-wu-tang-samurai-film-canon-shogun-assassin-ghost-dog-afro-samurai",
    "pillar": "anime",
    "title": "RZA Turned Samurai Cinema Into a Rap Cosmology",
    "headline": "RZA Sampled the Sword,\nThen Scored the Cartoon",
    "subhead": "From Shogun Assassin on Liquid Swords to the Ghost Dog score to Afro Samurai, Wu-Tang's producer spent three decades converting borrowed film into owned catalog.",
    "description": "How RZA moved from sampling Shogun Assassin on Liquid Swords to scoring Ghost Dog and Afro Samurai, and who owned each step.",
    "keywords": [
      "RZA",
      "Wu-Tang Clan",
      "Shogun Assassin",
      "Liquid Swords",
      "GZA",
      "Ghost Dog",
      "Jim Jarmusch",
      "Afro Samurai",
      "Wu Music Group",
      "Sacred Bones",
      "Lone Wolf and Cub",
      "sampling",
      "Sneakz and Beatz",
      "PHRHX",
      "Black-owned media"
    ],
    "ogHeadline": "RZA SAMPLED THE SWORD,\nTHEN SCORED THE CARTOON",
    "ogAccent": "F2C94C",
    "heroImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b1/RZA_speaking_at_the_2018_San_Diego_Comic_Con_International_%28cropped%29.jpg",
    "heroCredit": "Gage Skidmore / Wikimedia Commons",
    "heroCreditUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RZA_speaking_at_the_2018_San_Diego_Comic_Con_International_(cropped).jpg",
    "heroFocus": "center top",
    "trendScore": 55,
    "body": "RZA's relationship with Japanese swordplay cinema is usually filed under aesthetics. It is really a career-long argument about ownership: he started by borrowing other people's film audio without a budget, and he finished by getting paid to write the music those films would have used. Thirty years, three positions — sampler, composer, rights holder — and the through line is a Black producer moving up the value chain of an imported genre.\n\n## The sample era\n\nWu-Tang's foundational move was treating dubbed martial-arts film as source material with the same status as a soul break. The clan's mythology, its aliases, its skits and its title *Enter the Wu-Tang (36 Chambers)* all come out of Hong Kong kung fu cinema playing on New York television and in Times Square grindhouses.\n\nThe sharpest example is a samurai film, not a kung fu one. GZA's *Liquid Swords* (1995) opens with dialogue lifted from *Shogun Assassin*, the 1980 American recut of Japan's *Lone Wolf and Cub* films, and threads that child narration through the record. [The Quietus](https://thequietus.com/culture/film/shogun-assassin-liquid-swords-wu-tang-clan/) has written at length about why that pairing worked — a boy narrating his father's contract killings, laid over the coldest production RZA ever built. The film gave the album a moral frame Wu-Tang could not have written for itself.\n\nNone of that was a licensing partnership. It was a producer with a crate, a sampler and no clearance budget doing what the form required in 1995.\n\n## Ghost Dog moved him behind the desk\n\nIn 1999, Jim Jarmusch hired RZA to score [*Ghost Dog: The Way of the Samurai*](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghost_Dog:_The_Way_of_the_Samurai), with Forest Whitaker as a Black hitman in Jersey living by the *Hagakure*. The film is the thesis stated plainly: Black American street code and Japanese bushidō read as the same document, and RZA is the one scoring the overlap.\n\n[Slate](https://slate.com/culture/2020/11/ghost-dog-score-music-rza-jim-jarmusch.html) published a conversation between RZA and Jarmusch about that score, and the Sacred Bones reissue finally put the full instrumental work in print after two decades of it existing mostly as a soundtrack album with rap features attached. That is the shift worth marking: the sample-era producer became a film composer with a credit and a royalty stream.\n\n## Afro Samurai closed the loop\n\nThe last step was animation. RZA scored [*Afro Samurai*](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Afro_Samurai:_The_Album) in 2007 — a Japanese-produced series about a Black swordsman, built by Gonzo, executive produced by and starring Samuel L. Jackson — and released the companion album through Wu Music Group, his own imprint, in January 2007. He scored *Resurrection* in 2009 as well.\n\nRead that sequence in order. 1995: take audio from a Japanese film with no budget and no permission structure. 1999: get commissioned by an American auteur to score a film about that same code. 2007: get commissioned by a Japanese studio's American co-production to score an anime about a Black samurai, and own the record. The genre he raided as a fan eventually hired him as the author, and he kept the masters on the last one.\n\n## What the culture took from it\n\nWu-Tang's sword imagery is now so embedded that its origins get flattened into vibes. But the structure RZA built — a crew mythology borrowed from a foreign film canon, then made load-bearing — is the exact structure that later let a generation of rappers use *Naruto*, *Dragon Ball* and *Bleach* as shorthand for their own arcs. The pipeline runs through him. Before anyone was wearing an Akatsuki cloud on stage, RZA had already proven that imported Asian genre material could carry Black American autobiography without losing a thing in translation.\n\n## The take\n\nThe lesson here is not that RZA loved samurai movies. It's that he kept moving until he was the one being paid for the music instead of the one paying, quietly, in clearance risk. Sampling is a position with no equity. Scoring is a position with a credit. Owning the label that puts out the score is a position with a check that keeps arriving. Most producers stop at the first. He ran all three, and every anime-and-hip-hop deal signed since has been measured, consciously or not, against the standard he set.\n\n## Sources\n- [Shogun Assassin, GZA's Liquid Swords & The Sound Of Militant Dysphoria](https://thequietus.com/culture/film/shogun-assassin-liquid-swords-wu-tang-clan/) — The Quietus\n- [Ghost Dog score: RZA of Wu-Tang and Jim Jarmusch in conversation](https://slate.com/culture/2020/11/ghost-dog-score-music-rza-jim-jarmusch.html) — Slate, November 2020\n- [Ghost Dog: The Way of the Samurai](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghost_Dog:_The_Way_of_the_Samurai) — Wikipedia, accessed 2026\n- [Afro Samurai: The Album](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Afro_Samurai:_The_Album) — Wikipedia, accessed 2026\n- [RZA, SSGKobe, IDK, Robb Banks and Open Mike Eagle Explore Hip-Hop's Connection to Anime](https://www.xxlmag.com/hip-hop-anime/) — XXL\n",
