    "body": "Nobody has ever bought an Eminem Air Jordan 4 at retail, because there has never been a retail. That's not an accident of the era. It's the design. The 2005 Encore, the 2015 Carhartt and the 2017 Encore retro are the purest examples of a Jordan Brand product line that exists to be talked about rather than sold, and the numbers those pairs command at auction are the price of a story with no supply attached.\n\n## 2005: fifty pairs, no receipt\n\nThe Air Jordan 4 \"Encore\" was made in 2005 to mark the album, in a Blue Ribbon, Varsity Red, Stealth and Black nubuck, and [House of Heat](https://houseofheat.co/jordan/eminem-air-jordan-4-encore-2005-collab-history) puts the run at 50 pairs, gifted exclusively to friends and family. Eminem debuted them on Saturday Night Live, per [Sneaker Bar Detroit](https://sneakerbardetroit.com/eminem-air-jordan-4-encore/). The shoe followed no launch calendar and appeared in no store, and its market value was established entirely by the people who owned one deciding whether to let it go. A signed original sold at Sotheby's in December 2023 for $50,800.\n\nThe 2008 Air Jordan 2 \"The Way I Am\" followed the same model. Both shoes made Eminem, a white rapper from Detroit, the first musician with a multi-shoe Jordan Brand relationship built entirely on giveaways.\n\n## 2015: the Carhartt auction\n\nThe Carhartt Air Jordan 4 was the first time the model got a price tag, and it was an auction, not a release. Ten pairs, Carhartt canvas, a Shady XV logo and the hockey mask on the heel. [CBS Detroit](https://www.cbsnews.com/detroit/news/eminem-to-auction-off-10-pairs-of-exclusive-air-jordans-photo/) reported the auction opening November 10, 2015, to benefit the Marshall Mathers Foundation; Zac Dubasik's [Complex](https://www.complex.com/sneakers/a/zac-dubasik/eminem-air-jordan-4-carhartt-auction-results) tally of the eBay results has the money earmarked for Michigan State's Literacy Through Songwriting program.\n\nThe ten pairs sold for a combined $227,552, an average of $22,755 across 1,055 bids. A size 7 went for $18,750; a size 9 topped the list at $30,100. A shoe with no retail price and no resale history cleared $22,000 a pair on the strength of a name, a workwear brand and a charity.\n\n## 2017: twenty-three pairs and a $10 raffle\n\nThe Encore retro of December 2017 was rarer than the original: 23 pairs, per Sneaker Bar Detroit, released alongside the Revival album on December 15. StockX ran the distribution as a charity raffle, one entry per $10 donation, with net proceeds to the Marshall Mathers Foundation for Detroit programs. The retro kept the original look with a lighter grey on the heel tab and Wings. A size 12 later sold on StockX for $17,468 in May 2021, per House of Heat.\n\n## The myth economy\n\nThree releases, 83 pairs total, and a floor near $17,000. The Eminem 4s work because Jordan Brand never tested the demand: no raffle losers, no SNKRS \"Got 'Em\" screenshots, no Saturday morning line. The value is manufactured by absence, and the charity framing means nobody can call it a cash grab, because the cash went to Detroit literacy programs and Eminem's foundation.\n\nThat is the friends-and-family economy in full. It runs parallel to the retail calendar, it sets the aspirational ceiling for every regular collab, and it's why a Travis Scott \"friends and family\" purple 4 or a Union sample trades above the public pair. The public release is the product. The F&F pair is the advertisement for the product, and it's the advertisement that appreciates. Eminem's 4s are the extreme case because there is no public release to anchor them, only the auction results and the Sotheby's hammer.\n\n## The take\n\nJordan Brand's most reliable Black-culture partners, the boutiques, the rappers, the athletes, have always had to earn a public release. Eminem got three shoes with no release at all, and the shoes are worth more for it. There's a fairness question in that, and it isn't about Eminem, who used every pair to move money toward Detroit. It's about a system where the highest-value Jordan product is the one that skips the customer entirely.\n\nEvery F&F pair is a reminder of who Jordan Brand considers family. The list has grown since 2005, and it includes more Black owners than it did. But the model was built on a Detroit rapper's giveaways, and the numbers from that eBay auction in November 2015 are still the benchmark for what a Jordan is worth when nobody is allowed to buy it.\n\n## Sources\n- [Eminem's Air Jordan 4 \"Encore\" Was Almost Jordan Brand's First-Ever Collab](https://houseofheat.co/jordan/eminem-air-jordan-4-encore-2005-collab-history) — House of Heat, August 10, 2025\n- [Here's How Much Every Eminem x Carhartt x Air Jordan 4 Sold For on eBay](https://www.complex.com/sneakers/a/zac-dubasik/eminem-air-jordan-4-carhartt-auction-results) — Complex, November 24, 2015\n- [Eminem To Auction Off 10 Pairs Of Exclusive Air Jordans](https://www.cbsnews.com/detroit/news/eminem-to-auction-off-10-pairs-of-exclusive-air-jordans-photo/) — CBS News Detroit, November 3, 2015\n- [Eminem's Air Jordan 4 2017 Retro \"Encore\" Limited to 23 Pairs](https://sneakerbardetroit.com/eminem-air-jordan-4-encore/) — Sneaker Bar Detroit, December 13, 2017\n",
