    "slug": "hip-hop-estate-economy-who-controls-a-dead-rappers-catalog-2026",
    "pillar": "hiphop",
    "title": "Who Controls a Dead Rapper's Catalog",
    "headline": "Who Controls a Dead\nRapper's Catalog",
    "subhead": "Estates, trustees, probate courts and outside investors decide what happens to a catalog after the artist is gone. The artist almost never wrote the rules.",
    "description": "Tupac's Amaru, the Notorious B.I.G. catalog stake sold to Primary Wave, and how estates, trustees and investors control a rapper's music after death.",
    "keywords": [
      "music estates",
      "Amaru Entertainment",
      "Afeni Shakur",
      "Tupac Shakur",
      "Notorious B.I.G.",
      "Primary Wave",
      "catalog acquisition",
      "probate",
      "posthumous albums",
      "estate planning",
      "music rights",
      "publishing",
      "Sneakz and Beatz",
      "PHRHX",
      "Black-owned media"
    ],
    "ogHeadline": "WHO CONTROLS A DEAD\nRAPPER'S CATALOG",
    "ogAccent": "1A1A1A",
    "heroImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9f/TupacShakurWalkofFameStar.jpg",
    "heroCredit": "Wikimedia Commons",
    "heroCreditUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TupacShakurWalkofFameStar.jpg",
    "heroFocus": "center",
    "trendScore": 57,
    "body": "Rap has produced more posthumous catalogs than any genre of its age, and almost none of the artists left instructions. What fills that gap is not the culture and not the fans. It is probate law, a trustee's discretion, and whichever investment firm makes the estate an offer it cannot refuse.\n\n## The estate is a company, not a memory\n\nWhen an artist dies, the copyrights do not go to the audience or the collaborators. They go into an estate administered by an executor or into a trust run by a trustee, and that person makes the commercial decisions: what gets released, what gets licensed, what gets sold, and to whom.\n\nTwo control questions sit underneath everything. Who is the fiduciary, and what does the governing document let them do? A trust drafted with clear instructions constrains the trustee. An estate with no instructions hands enormous discretion to whoever the court appoints, and that discretion can outlive the beneficiaries' preferences by decades.\n\n## Amaru as the family-control model\n\nTupac's estate has operated through Amaru Entertainment, the company founded by his mother, Afeni Shakur, which became the vehicle for posthumous releases and the gatekeeper for his recordings and likeness. The structure kept release decisions inside the family rather than at a label, which is rare and was deliberate.\n\nIt is also the model with the highest dependency risk. Family-controlled estates are stable exactly as long as the family is aligned and the fiduciary is competent. When either fails, the resolution happens in probate court, in public, with the catalog's income as the prize.\n\n## The investor model\n\nThe other path is selling into a rights company. In March 2025, Primary Wave acquired a stake in the Notorious B.I.G.'s catalog — [Digital Music News](https://www.digitalmusicnews.com/2025/03/20/primary-wave-notorious-big-catalog-2/) and [Hypebeast](https://hypebeast.com/2025/3/primary-wave-music-acquires-50-percent-notorious-b-i-g-catalog-rights) reported the deal, [Consequence](https://consequence.net/2025/03/notorious-big-estate-catalog-rights-primary-wave/) covered it from the estate's side, and [Music Business Worldwide](https://www.musicbusinessworldwide.com/notorious-b-i-g-estate-nears-100m-catalog-deal-with-primary-wave-report1/) had reported the deal approaching nine figures beforehand. [Robb Report](https://robbreport.com/lifestyle/news/notorious-catalog-sale-deal-1236303150/) covered the valuation talk around it.\n\nWhat an estate buys with that transaction is liquidity now and a professional marketing partner. What it sells is a share of the upside forever, plus a seat at the table on how the catalog gets used. Rights companies are good at sync placements, anniversary campaigns, biopics and brand deals, because that is their business. They are also accountable to investors, and investor accountability and legacy stewardship do not always point the same direction.\n\n## The estates with no plan\n\nThe hardest cases are the artists who died young without documents. Disputes over who administers the estate, who the beneficiaries are, and what a personal representative may do become litigation, and the litigation costs come out of the catalog's earnings. XXXTentacion's estate has been the subject of exactly that kind of family dispute, reported by outlets including [Celebrity Net Worth](https://www.celebritynetworth.com/articles/celebrity/xxxtentacions-mother-sued-by-half-brother-for-11-million-over-late-rappers-estate/).\n\nRap's demographics make this a recurring structural problem rather than a series of individual tragedies. A genre whose stars are frequently in their twenties is a genre where estate planning is treated as somebody else's concern until the day it is the only concern.\n\n## The take\n\nThe culture argues endlessly about whether a posthumous album should exist. That argument is downstream of a document nobody read. Whether an unfinished verse becomes a single is decided by a fiduciary applying a trust instrument or a probate order — not by the artist's stated wishes, not by the fans, and not by the collaborators who were in the room.\n\nThe ownership lens is unavoidable here. Black artists generate catalogs that appreciate for fifty years, and the entities best positioned to buy those catalogs at the moment a family needs cash are investment firms with no connection to the community that made them. Every nine-figure estate deal is simultaneously a family securing its future and a permanent transfer of cultural equity out of the neighborhood.\n\nThe fix is boring and available: a will, a trust, a named successor manager, and written instructions about unreleased material. Do it at twenty-five, not at forty-five. The paperwork is the only version of your voice that gets a vote after you are gone.\n\n## Sources\n- [Primary Wave Officially Purchases Notorious B.I.G. Catalog Stake](https://www.digitalmusicnews.com/2025/03/20/primary-wave-notorious-big-catalog-2/) — Digital Music News, March 20, 2025\n- [Notorious B.I.G.'s Estate Sells Catalog Rights to Primary Wave](https://consequence.net/2025/03/notorious-big-estate-catalog-rights-primary-wave/) — Consequence, March 2025\n- [Notorious B.I.G. estate nears $100m+ music rights deal with Primary Wave](https://www.musicbusinessworldwide.com/notorious-b-i-g-estate-nears-100m-catalog-deal-with-primary-wave-report1/) — Music Business Worldwide\n- [Primary Wave Music Acquires Stake in The Notorious B.I.G.'s Catalog](https://hypebeast.com/2025/3/primary-wave-music-acquires-50-percent-notorious-b-i-g-catalog-rights) — Hypebeast, March 2025\n- [Notorious B.I.G.'s Music Catalog Could Sell for $150 Million](https://robbreport.com/lifestyle/news/notorious-catalog-sale-deal-1236303150/) — Robb Report\n",
    "publishedAt": "2026-11-11",
    "goLiveAt": "2026-11-11"
