    "ogHeadline": "PRODUCER MONEY\nAT THE 301 LEVEL",
    "ogAccent": "C8262C",
    "heroImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f1/16_bars_recording_studio.jpg",
    "heroCredit": "Le Creator via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0)",
    "heroCreditUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:16_bars_recording_studio.jpg",
    "heroFocus": "center",
    "trendScore": 54,
    "body": "A producer gets paid from three places, and most producers only know about one. The fee is the money up front. The points are a slice of the master. The publishing is a slice of the song. Beat leasing added a fourth stream in the 2010s that changed who gets to enter the business at all, and by November 2025 the biggest marketplace for it had paid out $400 million. Here is how each stream works, what the standard numbers are, and where the leverage sits.\n\n## Points: a percentage of what, exactly\n\nA point is nominally one percent of the sound-recording income. The [Music Admin guide](https://www.musicadmin.com/guides/how-do-producer-royalties-and-points-work/) makes the important correction: \"the percentage is only useful once the agreement explains what the point is being applied to.\" Three points can be three percent of the artist's royalty rate under a label deal, three percent of net receipts after distribution fees, or three points carved out of the artist's own pool in an all-in deal. Same number, three different checks.\n\nPoints usually pay from record one, meaning from the first sale, but only after the producer's own advance is recouped, and often not until the artist's recording costs are recouped too, depending on the paper. BeatStars Academy's [baseline standards](https://blog.beatstars.com/posts/baseline-standards-for-producer-agreements), published October 17, 2024, put the market range at two to five points, with five rare and some artists offering none. When points are off the table, the advice is to push the fee.\n\n## The fee, the split, the producer's share\n\nBeatStars' baseline puts a typical producer advance at $2,500 to $5,000, adjustable for the artist's status, the budget and how many producers touched the record. That advance is recoupable against points, which is why a producer on a flop keeps the fee and a producer on a hit eventually sees royalties.\n\nPublishing is the composition, and it is the stream labels cannot touch. The producer who built the beat is a co-writer of the song, and BeatStars' standard is that publishing \"should be split evenly,\" 50/50 between producer and artist, before any topline writers are added. That split gets documented on a split sheet at the session, and if it is not, the producer is arguing about it two years later with a lawyer. The Music Admin guide is clear that points cover the master only; the song needs its own paperwork.\n\nThe producer's share in the neighboring-rights world, the money SoundExchange collects from digital radio, is a separate letter-of-direction process, and it is where a lot of producer income goes unclaimed because nobody told them to file.\n\n## The lease economy\n\nBeat leasing inverted the order of operations. Instead of a producer being hired by a label to make a record, the producer makes the record first, posts it, and licenses it to whoever pays. A non-exclusive lease might cost $30 and permit a set number of streams; an exclusive costs more and takes the beat off the market. The producer retains the publishing on every version, and the paper is standardized by the platform rather than negotiated by a lawyer.\n\nThe proof of scale is [BeatStars](https://www.musicbusinessworldwide.com/beatstars-has-paid-creators-over-400m-ceo-abe-batshon-wants-1-million-musicians-to-earn-a-living-from-his-platform/). Music Business Worldwide reported on November 6, 2025 that the platform had paid creators $400 million to date, with 10 million creators, an 11-million-beat catalog and 1.5 million tracks downloaded a month. Lil Nas X's \"Old Town Road\" started as a BeatStars lease in 2019. Doechii's \"Denial Is a River,\" produced by Ian James, and Lil Tecca's \"Dark Thoughts,\" produced by Lucas Scharff, both came through the platform, and 82 tracks by BeatStars Publishing members had reached the Hot 100. CEO Abe Batshon's stated target is one million musicians earning a living, and the company's revenue is subscriptions and modest sales fees rather than a cut of the publishing.\n\n## What the lease changed\n\nTwo things, and they cut in opposite directions. First, the floor: a producer in any city with FL Studio can earn without a label, a manager or a session. That is new, and it is why the trap economy in Atlanta, Memphis and Detroit runs on people who never signed a producer agreement. Second, the ceiling: a $30 lease on a song that goes to No. 1 is a $30 lease, unless the paperwork upgrades it. The Old Town Road story ended well because the producer's publishing was intact and the industry came back to renegotiate. It does not always end that way, and the platform's terms, not the producer's lawyer, decide the first version of the deal.\n\n## The take\n\nThe reason to know this at the 301 level is that every era of hip-hop production has been an ownership story with a different villain. The SP-1200 generation got fees and no points. The Roc-A-Fella generation got points and paid publishers for samples. The FL Studio generation got the publishing and the platform. The money got closer to the producer each time, and the paperwork got more important, not less.\n\nDemand the split sheet at the session. Ask what the point is a point of. File the letter of direction. And treat a lease as a first offer, not a final one. The tools are cheap now. The contract is the instrument.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [How Do Producer Royalties and Points Work?](https://www.musicadmin.com/guides/how-do-producer-royalties-and-points-work/) — Music Admin, accessed September 2026\n- [Baseline Standards for Producer Agreements](https://blog.beatstars.com/posts/baseline-standards-for-producer-agreements) — BeatStars Academy, October 17, 2024\n- [BeatStars has paid creators over $400m to date](https://www.musicbusinessworldwide.com/beatstars-has-paid-creators-over-400m-ceo-abe-batshon-wants-1-million-musicians-to-earn-a-living-from-his-platform/) — Music Business Worldwide, November 6, 2025\n- [BeatStars](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BeatStars) — Wikipedia, accessed September 2026\n",
