    "ogHeadline": "HOW THE 990\nBECAME A CULTURE SHOE",
    "ogAccent": "C8102E",
    "heroImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/42/New_Balance_Women%27s_990_Running_Shoes.jpg",
    "heroCredit": "Wikimedia Commons",
    "heroCreditUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:New_Balance_Women%27s_990_Running_Shoes.jpg",
    "heroFocus": "center",
    "trendScore": 57,
    "body": "New Balance spent forty years being the shoe your uncle wore to the hardware store, and it was a good business. Then a designer from the West Side of Chicago and a brand from Queens did something Nike's marketing department would have charged tens of millions for: they made the 990 line mean something specific to people who had never considered the brand, and they did it with storytelling rather than technology.\n\n## Joe Freshgoods made the 990 a Chicago object\n\nJoe Robinson's work with New Balance did not treat the shoe as a canvas for graphics. It treated each release as a piece of writing about Black Chicago — family, memory, what it costs to leave and what it costs to stay. The 992 run, the 990v3 \"Outside Clothes\" project, the \"Aged Well\" concept: every one carried a thesis, and the packaging and campaign imagery did as much work as the upper.\n\n[Andscape's profile framed him as someone who made history with New Balance](https://andscape.com/features/joe-freshgoods-new-balance-992-aged-well-interview/) and then had to decide what to do with that position. [Complex covered the 990v3 \"Outside Clothes\" release](https://www.complex.com/sneakers/a/cmplxvictor-deng/joe-freshgoods-new-balance-990v3-outside-clothes-release-date) — a shoe built around the very specific ritual of clothes you only wear outside the house. [Soleretriever's interview around \"Aged Well\"](https://www.soleretriever.com/news/articles/aged-well-interview-joe-freshgoods-new-balance-2025) gets at the method: the concept comes first, the colorway serves it.\n\nHe also expanded the brand's silhouette range past the 990 family. [Essence covered his work on the 550 and the 2002R](https://www.essence.com/fashion/new-balance-conversations-among-us/), two models that have since become general-release staples in their own right.\n\n## Aimé Leon Dore rebuilt the brand's taste\n\nTeddy Santis came from a different angle — a Queens label built on a specific Greek-American, New York, prep-adjacent world — and New Balance eventually handed him creative direction of the Made in USA line. That is not a collab. That is a division. [Business of Fashion's interview with Santis](https://www.businessoffashion.com/articles/retail/aime-leon-dores-teddy-santis-on-new-balance-and-the-future-of-menswear/) covers the responsibility involved, and [WWD has tracked the drops](https://wwd.com/fashion-news/activewear/teddy-santis-new-balance-aime-leon-dore-1235675553/) as fashion news rather than sneaker news, which is exactly the repositioning New Balance wanted.\n\n[Soleretriever's history of the ALD partnership](https://www.soleretriever.com/news/articles/aime-leon-dore-new-balance-collaboration-history) shows how the 550 — a discontinued 1989 basketball shoe nobody was asking for — went from an ALD exclusive to one of the most widely worn shoes in the country.\n\nWhat makes the ALD arrangement unusual is its duration. Most creative-direction hires at footwear companies last a season or two and produce a capsule. Santis has been shaping an entire manufacturing line — the domestic-made program New Balance uses to justify its premium pricing — across years of seasonal deliveries, which means his decisions affect shoes that never carry his brand's name at all. That is influence over the catalog, not just over a collab.\n\n## The number at the end of it\n\nNew Balance is privately held, which usually means opacity, but the growth has been public enough. [FashionNetwork reported the company on course to reach $10 billion in revenue as early as 2026](https://ww.fashionnetwork.com/news/New-balance-on-course-to-reach-10-billion-in-revenue-as-early-as-2026,1809116.html). A brand that was a rounding error in the cultural conversation a decade ago is now competing for the same shelf space and the same customers as the Dunk.\n\n## The take\n\nThe interesting comparison is not New Balance versus Nike. It is how the two companies treat the people who make them relevant. Nike's model is the collab: a season, a royalty, the tooling stays home. New Balance handed a design director title and a production line to an outside brand, and gave a Chicago designer enough rope to tell multi-year stories about his own neighborhood on a running shoe. Neither designer owns New Balance. But one of these companies is visibly building long relationships with Black and immigrant creative voices instead of renting them by the drop, and the revenue curve suggests the market noticed. The 990 was never going to become a culture shoe through engineering. It became one because somebody was finally allowed to say what it meant.\n\n## Sources\n- [Joe Freshgoods made history with New Balance, but what's his next act?](https://andscape.com/features/joe-freshgoods-new-balance-992-aged-well-interview/) — Andscape\n- [Joe Freshgoods' Latest New Balance Collab Is Releasing Again](https://www.complex.com/sneakers/a/cmplxvictor-deng/joe-freshgoods-new-balance-990v3-outside-clothes-release-date) — Complex\n- [Aimé Leon Dore's Teddy Santis on New Balance and the Future of Menswear](https://www.businessoffashion.com/articles/retail/aime-leon-dores-teddy-santis-on-new-balance-and-the-future-of-menswear/) — The Business of Fashion\n- [The Complete History of Aimé Leon Dore x New Balance](https://www.soleretriever.com/news/articles/aime-leon-dore-new-balance-collaboration-history) — Soleretriever\n- [New Balance on course to reach $10 billion in revenue as early as 2026](https://ww.fashionnetwork.com/news/New-balance-on-course-to-reach-10-billion-in-revenue-as-early-as-2026,1809116.html) — FashionNetwork\n",
